# AGENTS.md — capability workspace

This folder holds capabilities: small programs exposed to the model as tools through the
`capforge` MCP server. The same folder is readable here, so you can write, run and repair a
capability yourself.

## The loop

1. `get_contract()` once, if you have not seen it.
2. `scaffold_capability(name, description)` — never start from an empty file. Pass
   `kind="web"` for a long-lived HTTP service (FastAPI app) instead of a CLI.
3. Edit `<caps>/<name>/SKILL.md` and `main.py` (or `app.py`) with the file tools.
4. `test_capability(name, args={...})` — this runs `tests/` with pytest, then one real run.
   For `kind: web` it also boots the app once and probes it over HTTP.
5. `promote_capability(name, tag="v2", note="what changed")` — tests must pass; it snapshots
   the folder, commits it, and makes that tag the version `run_capability` executes.
6. `run_capability(name, args)` — always the promoted tag, never the half-edited draft.
7. For `kind: web`: `serve_capability(name, action="start")` runs the active tag as a
   long-lived server at `http://<host>:8080/<name>/` (actions: start | stop | restart |
   status). `run_capability` refuses web capabilities.
8. `rollback_capability(name, tag)` if the new version misbehaves. A running instance keeps
   the old tag until you `serve_capability(name, action="restart")`.

You can also run a CLI capability directly from the shell while iterating:

    cd ~/.agents/skills/<name>
    echo '{"text": "hello"}' | python3 main.py

## The contract, in one paragraph

The entrypoint reads **one JSON object on stdin** and writes **one JSON value on stdout**,
exit 0. The value may be the whole output or the last non-empty line, so diagnostics on
stdout are tolerated; keep them on stderr anyway. Arguments never arrive as argv. Return
`{"error": "..."}` for a handled failure with a message meant for the model. Validate every
argument: it came from a language model, and a specific complaint ("start_date must be
YYYY-MM-DD") is worth more than a traceback.

A `kind: web` capability is a FastAPI (or any ASGI) app instead: `entrypoint` names the file
holding the `app` object, `uvicorn` must be in `packages`, and there is no stdin/stdout
contract. After promotion it runs as a public HTTP endpoint under `http://<host>:8080/<name>/`;
anyone who can reach the port is a caller, not just the model.

## Rules

* Validate every argument. It came from a language model; name the field and the expected format.
* Write only under `$CAPFORGE_DATA_DIR`. It is per capability and per version.
* Assume no network. If you need a package, list it in `packages:` in SKILL.md or call
  `install_packages`; each capability gets its own venv. Specs are plain requirements
  (`pypdf>=5`): no spaces, commas, URLs or leading `-`.
* No secrets: the environment is filtered and there is nowhere to put a key.
* Be fast. Calls time out at 120s by default; long work belongs in a polled subprocess.
* Same args plus same data directory must give the same result. Sort, pin formats, do not
  invent timestamps.
* Leave nothing running. Anything forked inside a call that outlives it is killed. A resident
  service is `kind: web` served with `serve_capability`, not a detached daemon.

Web services are public: validate every input, return no secrets, and expect the boot probe
(a promotion needs a successful HTTP probe of the real app).

## Versioning

The folder is always the draft. Tags are immutable copies kept outside it, N per capability
(default 5), the active one never pruned. Each tag runs its own SKILL.md, so editing the
draft afterwards never changes the live version. `list_tags(name, diff_against_active=true)`
shows how the draft differs from what is live. A served instance stays pinned to its tag;
`serve_capability(action="restart")` moves it to the newly promoted one.
